  "worked_solution": "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
